  "worked_solution": "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{\u2033}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{\u2033}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}\u2061(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}\u2061(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,\u27f9\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}\u2061(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}\u2061(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$",
